course: dictionary
lesson: 5420
title: Lad and friendly words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A lad is a boy or a young man. It is an informal word, so you hear it in everyday talk and stories, especially in the UK, Ireland and Australia. «At tea Grandad says: "Being frightened means you're a coward, lad."» Here lad is a friendly way of talking to a boy, a bit like saying "son" or "mate".
explain: Look at the words around lad to find its meaning. «The lads» means a group of male friends, so it is more than one person. «Well done, lad!» shows warmth and praise. Ask yourself: is the speaker being friendly, or describing someone? The clues tell you which.
explain: Lad can also mean something less kind. In UK slang, a lad or laddish person is a loud young man who acts tough or rough. «In Liverpool a big lad told Ben, "Swap you my phone for your bike..."» The word big and the pushy trade tell you this lad is acting rough. The same word can be warm in one sentence and rough in another, so context decides.
fact: A lad is a boy or young man, and the word is informal.
fact: «The lads» means a group of male friends.
fact: «Lad» can be a friendly way of talking to a boy, as in «Well done, lad!».
fact: In UK slang, a «laddish» person is a loud young man who acts tough.
fact: Regional words: «la» (Liverpool) means lad; «boss» (Liverpool) means brilliant; «our lad» (northern England) means my son or brother.
exercise: «At tea Grandad says: "Being frightened means you're a coward, lad."» In this sentence, lad means: | *A friendly way of talking to a boy | A loud young man who acts tough | A worker with horses | A group of male friends
exercise: Ben says, «The lads are going to the park after school.» What does the lads mean? | A single boy with a bike | A stable worker | A rough, loud young man | *A group of male friends
exercise: In Liverpool, what does «la» mean? | *Lad | Petrol station | Thanks | Brilliant
test: «Well done, lad!» Which word is lad doing in this sentence? | *A friendly way of talking to a boy | A word for a horse worker | A word for a loud, rough man | A name for a group of friends
test: Which sentence shows lad as slang for a loud young man who acts tough? | *«The big lad shouted at everyone and pushed past the queue.» | «The lads are kicking a ball in the field.» | «Well done, lad, you finished first!» | «Our lad helps Mum with the washing.»
exam: Read «The lad who was always trouble stood alone by the gate.» What does lad mean here, and what clue tells you? | A stable worker, because gate is a farm word | *A boy or young man, because the clue is always trouble, which describes him | A friendly greeting, because the sentence is about a gate | A group of friends, because stood alone shows a crowd
